    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Investigate a sparse-obstacle approach, but prioritize reducing overhead and validating it against the measured baseline: all four measured sparse inclusion-exclusion variants are slower (0.109–0.143 s) than the dense baseline (0.081 s). Profile likely causes and benchmark alternatives such as a compact flat bitmap plus row-wise DP, avoiding an N×N vector-of-vectors allocation where appropriate. Preserve correctness for arbitrary valid blocked coordinates, duplicates, and blocked endpoints; do not assume sparse is faster merely from asymptotic complexity.",
        "B": "Explore a distinct optimization: retain row-wise dynamic programming while exploiting sparse obstacles, e.g. process only affected rows/segments or use a flat representation, and compare against baseline and A/C proposals. The observed O(m²) obstacle inclusion-exclusion variants are slower than baseline on this run, so avoid adopting them without measured gains. Ensure every valid input, including duplicates and blocked start/destination, matches the reference.",
        "C": "Study the measured bottleneck and test low-risk implementations of the original O(N²) DP: flat blocked-cell storage or more efficient input/row handling, with correctness preserved. The sparse factorial/inclusion-exclusion candidates all regressed in measured time; investigate whether sorting, pairwise obstacle scans, or factorial setup dominates rather than presuming that method will win. Return only evidence-backed improvements.",
        "G": "Integrate only candidate programs produced by A/B/C, preserving correctness and source ownership. Benchmark each candidate and the combined integration on the official harness; the baseline is correct at 0.08076 s, while observed sparse candidates are slower (0.10892, 0.11926, 0.12158, 0.14338 s). Do not combine changes assuming additive gains: retain the baseline unless a complete integrated measurement demonstrates a correct improvement. Respect the fixed target (ARM64, 4 cores) and all constraints.",
        "analysis": "Problem D’s baseline is correct and runs in 0.08076 s. The four admissible programs all replace dense grid DP with sorted sparse obstacles, factorial tables, and pairwise inclusion-exclusion; their measurements (0.10892–0.14338 s) are slower. Their O(m²) pair scans plus sorting and setup may outweigh memory savings on the tested input; the measurements do not establish why. Research should therefore emphasize measured profiling and alternative implementations rather than blindly repeating the same transformation. The matrix dimension can reach nearly 200,000, making dense O(N²) storage/work risky, while the stated sparse-obstacle guarantee applies for large N; any alternative must remain exact for all valid inputs, including duplicates and blocked endpoints. Integration must evaluate complete programs because gains need not combine."
      },
